<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
    <artifactId>mainframe-parent</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>mainframe-dist</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <name>MainFrame Distributable</name>
  <description>
    One mainframe.exe: the stock shell, and the programs it opens in it.
  </description>

  <!--
    Why this module is not in the parent's default module list.

    The other two modules are libraries and answer to nobody: mainframe-core compiles on a machine that has
    never heard of vexelray, and mainframe-vexel-gui compiles against the graphics stack and nothing above it.
    This one is the opposite. It is a leaf, and it names applications, so building it needs the calculator and
    the editor installed. Listing it as an ordinary module would make a fresh clone of MainFrame unbuildable
    without them, which trades the parent's one real invariant for a convenience. So it lives behind -Pdist,
    and the ordinary build stays exactly what it was.

    The arrow direction is the point and it is deliberate: an app depends on mainframe-vexel-gui and never the
    reverse, and this module is the one place allowed to know both ends. Nothing in here is a library. If
    something here turns out to be reusable it belongs in mainframe-vexel-gui instead.
  -->

  <properties>
    <main.class>dev.mainframe.dist.Dist</main.class>
    <calculator.version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</calculator.version>
    <text-editor.version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</text-editor.version>
    <!-- One token each, because a Maven property is not a command line. Blank slots are dropped. -->
    <app.args></app.args>
    <app.args2></app.args2>
  </properties>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- MainFrame as a window, and the shared boot every application built this way uses. Brings
         mainframe-core, the shell itself, in behind it. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
      <artifactId>mainframe-vexel-gui</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- The assistant: `ask` and `key`. Not an application and not a window, which is why it arrives through
         ConsoleApp.of rather than as one of the programs below.

         This is the one dependency in the whole tree with a transitive closure worth naming: OkHttp, Jackson
         and the Kotlin standard library, arriving behind the Anthropic SDK. Everything MainFrame is built out
         of otherwise has none, and native-image has to be told about every one of them, but not at build
         time, which is the trap. The image links happily without a word about the SDK and then throws on the
         first question, because Jackson reaches the SDK's own deserialisers reflectively. They are registered
         in META-INF/native-image/dev.mainframe/mainframe-dist-assistant/, the third metadata file; the README
         says how it was traced and what that trace does not cover. Claude.java is the seam where a
         hand-written HTTP client would go if the weight ever stops being worth it. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
      <artifactId>mainframe-assistant</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- Starting a project from a template: `templates`, and `new vexel-desktop`. In, rather than out
         like the assistant was, because it costs nothing to be in: no third-party jar, no HTTP client,
         nothing for native-image to be taught beyond the two resource globs the module carries in its own
         reachability-metadata.json. The template's files are resources, so the exe writes a whole VexelRay
         project with nothing installed beside it. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.mainframe</groupId>
      <artifactId>mainframe-template</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>

    <!-- The two programs this distributable carries. Each is an ordinary ConsoleApp out of its own
         repository: nothing was added to either one for the sake of being shipped here. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.vexelray.demo</groupId>
      <artifactId>calculator-vexel-demo</artifactId>
      <version>${calculator.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>dev.vexelray.demo</groupId>
      <artifactId>text-editor-vexel-demo</artifactId>
      <version>${text-editor.version}</version>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <!-- mainframe.jar and mainframe(.exe): that name is on the command line and in muscle memory. -->
    <finalName>mainframe</finalName>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <manifest>
              <mainClass>${main.class}</mainClass>
            </manifest>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
      <!-- mvn -Pdist -pl mainframe-dist compile exec:exec  ->  the same program on the JVM, for when the
           question is whether MainFrame works rather than whether native-image can hold it. -->
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
        <artifactId>exec-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <executable>${java.home}/bin/java</executable>
          <classpathScope>runtime</classpathScope>
          <arguments>
            <argument>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</argument>
            <argument>-classpath</argument>
            <classpath/>
            <argument>${main.class}</argument>
            <argument>${app.args}</argument>
            <argument>${app.args2}</argument>
          </arguments>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>

  <profiles>
    <!-- The native backends, chosen by the host OS. The app modules declare these in profiles of their own,
         and a profile does not travel with a dependency, so they are named again here. This is the module
         being built, so this is where they belong. -->
    <profile>
      <id>platform-windows</id>
      <activation><os><family>windows</family></os></activation>
      <dependencies>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
          <artifactId>vexelray-os-windows</artifactId>
          <version>${vexelray.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>sibarum.tactroller</groupId>
          <artifactId>tactroller-windows</artifactId>
          <version>${tactroller.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
      </dependencies>
    </profile>
    <profile>
      <id>platform-linux</id>
      <activation><os><family>unix</family><name>linux</name></os></activation>
      <dependencies>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
          <artifactId>vexelray-os-linux</artifactId>
          <version>${vexelray.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>sibarum.tactroller</groupId>
          <artifactId>tactroller-linux</artifactId>
          <version>${tactroller.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
      </dependencies>
    </profile>
    <profile>
      <id>platform-macos</id>
      <activation><os><family>mac</family></os></activation>
      <dependencies>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>dev.vexelray</groupId>
          <artifactId>vexelray-os-macos</artifactId>
          <version>${vexelray.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
          <groupId>sibarum.tactroller</groupId>
          <artifactId>tactroller-macos</artifactId>
          <version>${tactroller.version}</version>
          <scope>runtime</scope>
        </dependency>
      </dependencies>
    </profile>

    <!-- mvn -Pdist,native package  ->  mainframe-dist/target/mainframe(.exe) -->
    <profile>
      <id>native</id>
      <build>
        <plugins>
          <plugin>
            <!-- native-image compiles small probe executables and runs them to measure the platform. Windows
                 Application Control blocks a brand-new unsigned .exe under %TEMP%, so the probes are pointed
                 at target/nitmp instead, which this makes sure exists. Same reason as mainframe-core. -->
            <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
            <artifactId>maven-antrun-plugin</artifactId>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>make-native-temp-dir</id>
                <phase>prepare-package</phase>
                <goals><goal>run</goal></goals>
                <configuration>
                  <target>
                    <mkdir dir="${project.build.directory}/nitmp"/>
                  </target>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
              <!-- The executable's own icon, which is a packaging fact rather than an engine one: what
                   Explorer shows for mainframe.exe, and what a pinned shortcut shows before the program runs.
                   The icon of the *running* application is the other half and lives in Mark.java, which
                   Desktop hands to setApplicationIcon. Compiling .rc -> .res needs rc.exe on PATH, which the
                   MSVC environment native-image already requires on Windows supplies; failonerror, because an
                   icon that silently did not get linked looks exactly like one that was never asked for. -->
              <execution>
                <id>compile-exe-icon</id>
                <phase>prepare-package</phase>
                <goals><goal>run</goal></goals>
                <configuration>
                  <target>
                    <exec executable="rc" failonerror="true">
                      <arg value="/nologo"/>
                      <arg value="/fo"/>
                      <arg file="${project.build.directory}/mainframe.res"/>
                      <arg file="${project.basedir}/src/main/native/mainframe.rc"/>
                    </exec>
                  </target>
                </configuration>
              </execution>
            </executions>
          </plugin>
          <plugin>
            <groupId>org.graalvm.buildtools</groupId>
            <artifactId>native-maven-plugin</artifactId>
            <extensions>true</extensions>
            <executions>
              <execution>
                <id>build-native</id>
                <goals><goal>compile-no-fork</goal></goals>
                <phase>package</phase>
              </execution>
            </executions>
            <configuration>
              <imageName>mainframe</imageName>
              <mainClass>${main.class}</mainClass>
              <buildArgs>
                <buildArg>--no-fallback</buildArg>
                <buildArg>-H:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions</buildArg>
                <!-- The graphics stack and both input backends are Panama downcalls, and Win32 calls back in
                     through an upcall stub for the window procedure. Both need FFM support switched on; the
                     signatures themselves are registered in reachability-metadata.json. -->
                <buildArg>-H:+ForeignAPISupport</buildArg>
                <!-- The input backend hands each raw-input registration its own Arena.ofShared, because the
                     window procedure that reads it runs on a different thread from the one that opened it.
                     native-image leaves shared arenas off by default, and without this the whole application
                     runs and then throws on the way out, when the backend closes them. -->
                <buildArg>-H:+SharedArenaSupport</buildArg>
                <buildArg>--enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED</buildArg>
                <buildArg>-H:+ReportExceptionStackTraces</buildArg>
                <buildArg>-J-Djava.io.tmpdir=${project.build.directory}/nitmp</buildArg>
                <!--
                  No console window.

                  native-image links a console-subsystem executable, which on Windows means the loader hands
                  the process a console before anything runs: a black window appears next to MainFrame, and
                  because that console owns the process group, closing it kills the application. For a CLI that
                  is exactly right, which is why mainframe-core keeps it. This is a windowed program, so it
                  says so at link time instead.

                  /ENTRY is not optional alongside it: /SUBSYSTEM:WINDOWS makes the linker look for WinMain,
                  and the image's entry point is an ordinary main, so the C runtime startup that calls main has
                  to be named explicitly or the link fails with an unresolved WinMain.

                  What this costs is only the console the process would have been given: output still arrives
                  wherever the launcher provides a handle, so running it from a shell pipeline still prints.
                  Double-clicked there is nowhere for it to go, which means a crash before the window appears
                  is silent. Nothing here depends on it either way, because the shell writes to its own
                  scrollback rather than to a stream. For output while developing, run the JVM arrangement:
                  see exec:exec above.
                -->
                <buildArg>-H:NativeLinkerOption=/SUBSYSTEM:WINDOWS</buildArg>
                <buildArg>-H:NativeLinkerOption=/ENTRY:mainCRTStartup</buildArg>
                <!-- The compiled icon resource, handed to the linker like any other object. Built by the
                     compile-exe-icon execution above; a Windows linker option beside the two above it. -->
                <buildArg>-H:NativeLinkerOption=${project.build.directory}/mainframe.res</buildArg>
              </buildArgs>
            </configuration>
          </plugin>
        </plugins>
      </build>
    </profile>
  </profiles>
</project>
